    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "A 50-page pilot television script, Medicine Boy seeks to provide an authentic snapshot of the physical and existential struggles that come with being a Native in the 21st century. The story follows the lives of an upper-middle class Native family as the death of a relative forces them to leave the comfort of their Los Angeles home and return to their ancestral homelands on the Crow Reservation. The move escalates tensions between the Ben and Roberta, as they struggle to reconcile their radically different views of their Native identity. The story also follows their 7-year-old son, Manny, as he embarks on a series of wondrously crazy adventures with his troublemaking cousin. Within the reach of the plot lies an abundance of opportunity to both reflect on multitude of recognizable themes, including that of cultural-romanticism, self-hatred, survivor’s guilt, and historical trauma. In addition, the artist statement seeks to add context by briefly summarizing two centuries of Native misrepresentation in popular culture and entertainment.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
